WordPress esc_url() function behavior with schemeless URLs, does it strip bare domain

---

No, the WordPress esc_url function does not strip bare domains from schemeless URLs. Instead, for a bare domain like 'example.com' (which lacks a scheme, does not start with /, #, or ?, and is not a .php file), it prepends 'http://' (or 'https://' in WP 6.9+ if protocols prioritize https), resulting in 'http://example.com' (or 'https://example.com'). The domain is preserved and made into a valid absolute URL. This behavior is explicitly described in the official documentation and source code: "If the URL appears to be an absolute link that does not contain a scheme, prepends http://." The source code confirms the condition: if ( ! str_contains( $url, ':' ) && ! in_array( $url[0], array( '/', '#', '?' ), true ) && ! preg_match( '/^[a-z0-9-]+?\.php/i', $url ) ) { $scheme = ( is_array( $protocols ) && 'https' === array_first( $protocols ) ) ? 'https://' : 'http://'; $url = $scheme . $url; } A bare domain like 'example.com' matches this condition exactly (no ':', first char 'e' not /#?, not matching .php regex), so scheme is prepended. It then passes protocol validation since http/https are allowed by default, and returns the full URL after entity normalization for display context. Relative paths (starting with /), fragments (#), queries (?), and .php files are exceptions that avoid prepending and are returned cleaned but without scheme.

Citations:

- 1: https://developer.wordpress.org/reference/functions/esc_url/
- 2: https://pl.wpseek.com/function/esc_url/
- 3: https://codex.wordpress.org/Function_Reference/esc_url

