body:
_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_ | _⚡ Quick win_

**Tighten test cleanup to avoid global state leakage**

Line 516 removes *all* `wp_redirect` filters, which can break unrelated tests. This test also keeps the elevated current user after it finishes. Use callback-specific removal and reset the user in `finally`.

<details>
<summary>Safer cleanup pattern</summary>

```diff
-		add_filter(
-			'wp_redirect',
-			function ($location) use (&$redirect_url) {
-				$redirect_url = $location;
-
-				throw new \RuntimeException('redirect_intercepted');
-			}
-		);
+		$redirect_filter = static function ($location) use (&$redirect_url) {
+			$redirect_url = $location;
+			throw new \RuntimeException('redirect_intercepted');
+		};
+		add_filter('wp_redirect', $redirect_filter);
@@
-		} finally {
-			remove_all_filters('wp_redirect');
+		} finally {
+			remove_filter('wp_redirect', $redirect_filter);
+			wp_set_current_user(0);
 		}
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
Verify each finding against current code. Fix only still-valid issues, skip the
rest with a brief reason, keep changes minimal, and validate.

In `@tests/WP_Ultimo/Admin_Pages/Settings_Admin_Page_Test.php` around lines 493 -
519, The test currently uses remove_all_filters to clean up the wp_redirect
hook, which removes all filters added by any code and can break unrelated tests.
Instead, store the filter callback in a variable so you can remove only your
specific callback using remove_filter with the callback reference. Additionally,
the test elevates the current user to super admin with add_cap in the setup but
never resets it, causing global state leakage. Add wp_set_current_user(0) in the
finally block to reset the user state after the test completes.
```

</details>

