_🚀 Performance & Scalability_ | _🟠 Major_ | _🏗️ Heavy lift_

**`file_get_contents()` loads the entire SQL dump into memory — risk of OOM on large imports.**

Full-site exports can produce SQL dumps of hundreds of MB or more. Reading the whole file at once here can exhaust memory and fail the import. Notably, the sibling `replace_db_prefix()` in this same class was deliberately written to stream line-by-line "to avoid loading the entire file into memory" — this helper should follow the same pattern, especially since it runs on the same (potentially huge) file.

A streaming scan also avoids matching table-name-like tokens that happen to appear inside large `INSERT` value blocks.




<details>
<summary>♻️ Streaming alternative</summary>

```diff
 	private function get_imported_table_names($filename) {
-
-		$contents = file_get_contents($filename);
-
-		if ( ! is_string($contents) || '' === $contents ) {
-			return [];
-		}
-
-		preg_match_all('/(?:CREATE TABLE(?: IF NOT EXISTS)?|INSERT INTO|LOCK TABLES)\s+`?([A-Za-z0-9_]+)`?/i', $contents, $matches);
-
-		if ( empty($matches[1]) ) {
-			return [];
-		}
-
-		return array_values(array_unique($matches[1]));
+		$handle = fopen($filename, 'r');
+
+		if ( false === $handle ) {
+			return [];
+		}
+
+		$tables = [];
+
+		while ( false !== ($line = fgets($handle)) ) {
+			if ( preg_match('/^\s*(?:CREATE TABLE(?: IF NOT EXISTS)?|INSERT INTO|LOCK TABLES)\s+`?([A-Za-z0-9_]+)`?/i', $line, $m) ) {
+				$tables[ $m[1] ] = true;
+			}
+		}
+
+		fclose($handle);
+
+		return array_keys($tables);
 	}
```
</details>

Note: anchoring with `^\s*` (statement start) also reduces false matches from table-like tokens embedded in row data.

