`WordPress remove_all_filters removes all filters regardless priority hook`

💡 Result:

The remove_all_filters function in WordPress does not always remove all filters regardless of priority; its behavior depends entirely on the arguments provided [1][2]. By default, the function is designed to remove all callbacks associated with a specified hook [1][2]. However, it accepts an optional $priority parameter [1][3]. 1. If the $priority parameter is omitted (or set to false, which is the default), the function removes all callbacks registered to that hook, regardless of their priority [1][3]. 2. If a specific integer priority is provided, the function will only remove the callbacks registered at that specific priority level, leaving callbacks at other priority levels intact [1][3][4]. In summary, the function provides the flexibility to either clear an entire hook or target specific priority levels for removal [1][3]. Sources: [1] https://developer.wordpress.org/reference/functions/remove_all_filters/ [2] https://developer.wordpress.org/reference/functions/remove_all_filters/?output_format=md [3] https://developer.wordpress.org/reference/classes/wp_hook/remove_all_filters/ [4] https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/6.8.1/src/wp-includes/class-wp-hook.php

