body:
**🎯 Functional Correctness** | **🟡 Minor** | **⚡ Quick win**

**A failed handoff breaks the ready state. Fall back to the existing redirect instead of throwing.**

When the handoff is enabled and `requestCheckoutHandoff()` returns `""` or throws, line 174 throws. The `catch` block then does the following:

- It increments `failures`.
- It polls again. Each new poll asks for another handoff and inserts another nonce row.
- After five failures, `show("waiting", true)` replaces the "ready" message. The customer then sees "Setup is taking longer than expected" for a site that is ready.

This also discards `data.redirect_url`, so the integration redirect fallback stops working. The PR states that this fallback is kept.

A realistic trigger: an operator sets `redirect_to_site` while magic links are disabled. In that case `generate_checkout_handoff_url()` always returns `false`, and the endpoint always returns HTTP 409.

Fall back to `data.redirect_url`, or stay on the ready page, when the handoff is unavailable.

<details>
<summary>🐛 Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
--- "a/assets/js/checkout-provisioning.js"
+++ "b/assets/js/checkout-provisioning.js"
@@ -166,14 +166,15 @@
 					return;
 				}
 				if (state === "ready") {
 					let redirect_url = data.redirect_url;

 					if (isCheckoutHandoffEnabled()) {
-						redirect_url = await requestCheckoutHandoff();
-						if (! redirect_url) {
-							throw new Error("Checkout handoff unavailable");
-						}
+						try {
+							redirect_url = (await requestCheckoutHandoff()) || data.redirect_url;
+						} catch {
+							redirect_url = data.redirect_url;
+						}
 					}

 					if (redirect_url) {
 						const url = new URL(redirect_url, location.href);
```
</details>

